# The build context is now the repo root (it used to be ./backend and
# ./frontend/serverctl separately). Docker only reads the .dockerignore at the
# context root, so the ones inside backend/ and frontend/serverctl/ no longer
# apply to anything and this file replaces both.
#
# Two things go wrong without it. The context sent to the daemon grows by ~60 MB
# of virtualenv on every build, and — worse — `COPY frontend/serverctl/ ./`
# overlays a host-installed node_modules on top of the `npm ci` result from the
# layer above. install.py runs `npm install` in exactly that directory, so anyone
# who followed the local-development instructions would build the image against
# their host's resolved dependency tree instead of the lockfile's.
#
# NOTE: these are not .gitignore patterns. Docker matches each pattern against
# the whole path relative to the context root, and a plain `node_modules` matches
# only a top-level one — nested paths need the `**/` prefix. Getting this wrong
# fails silently: the build still works, it just copies everything.

# Secrets. backend/.env is nested, hence **/ — it holds the admin password hash
# and JWT signing key, and the context is a copy that leaves the host.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.secret

# Python
**/myenv
**/venv
**/.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]

# Node — must be resolved from the lockfile inside the image, never copied in
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/out
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Not referenced by any COPY in the Dockerfile
.git
.gitignore
.github
**/*.md
nginx
docker-compose.yml
install.py
setup.py
preflight.py
selftest.py

# Editor / OS noise
.vscode
.idea
**/*.swp
**/.DS_Store
